&quot;A legacy of distorted citations: Critical deconstruction of attribution errors in the Shannon-Wiener Diversity Index across Indonesian and global ecological literature&quot;

Abstract
The Shannon-Wiener diversity index (H&#039;) is among the most widely used biostatistical parameters in ecological studies worldwide. Yet a critical, historically grounded examination of the primary literature underlying its application exposes a chain of attribution errors that have been massively replicated for more than five decades. This commentary identifies, documents, and deconstructs three principal errors: (1) the unresolved dual nomenclature of &#039;Wiener&#039; versus &#039;Weaver&#039; in the global scientific community—directly addressed by Spellerberg &amp; Fedor (2003) in Global Ecology and Biogeography—which continues to propagate in peer-reviewed journals including a Springer publication (Arias-González et al., 2012) and a 2025 agricultural science article (Balagoni Maruthi et al., 2025); (2) the systematic misattribution of the species evenness index (J&#039;) to Odum (1971) rather than its originator Pielou (1966); and (3) the erroneous universal application of environmental quality thresholds (H&#039; &lt;1, 1–3, &gt;3) that were calibrated empirically by Wilhm &amp; Dorris (1968) exclusively for macrozoobenthos communities in organically polluted lotic waters. We further incorporate the fundamental mathematical critique by Jost (2006) in Oikos—that H&#039; is an entropy, not a true diversity measure—and the empirical bias documentation from Ecological Indicators (2016), showing that H&#039; is systematically distorted by species richness in ways that invalidate inter-community comparisons. The transmission pathway of these misconceptions in Indonesia is traced through Tjahjono Samingan&#039;s translated textbook (1993) and the phenomenon of &#039;black-box bias&#039; in statistical software. The article closes with concrete recommendations for journal editorial boards, higher education institutions, and the ecological research community to restore standards of biostatistical attribution.
